@arc home.jo
who: home:jo
name: Jo
pron: they
// Writer 3's arc, rewritten as ONE-SIDED CALLS by head writer 4 (the user, 2026-10-17 evening: one call a day, you
// choose who; only they speak; no Letters night). Jo, your older sibling (they / them), stuck in a dull job, proud of
// you and a bit envious, quits to apprentice at a boatyard, and launches their first boat.
//
// HOW THEY TALK: dry, teasing, big-sibling; short sentences, long pauses; calls you "kid" (always, at any age) or {me}.
//   Jokes first, means it second. Says the honest thing late at night. One-sided suits them: "Don't interrupt. You
//   can't. Brilliant."
// NEVER: bitter at you (the envy is about them, not you, and they say so); guilt-trips; genders you.
// The job is checking forms at the freight office ("the office"; Steve the stapler); the boatyard is down by the water
//   at home (from beat 2); the old boatwright is "Wick" (they / them, from beat 4); the first boat is a small rowing
//   boat Jo names the Little Wren (beat 6). Jo waters Gran's marrow after her fall: only behind beat:home.gran.5.
// RUNNING: "kid"; Steve the stapler; sawdust everywhere; the Little Wren.

// ---- the arc ----
== beat 1
tone: light
mem: firstcall
flag: home.jo.called
them: Kid. It's Jo. Mum says you're on a spaceship. I said I'll believe it when it rings me.   [laugh]
them: Then Mum said I have to ring you, because you can't ring us. So here I am. Believing it.
them: So. How is it? Is it all buttons? I bet it's all buttons. Don't touch the red ones.
them: Work is forms. Then more forms. Then lunch. Then forms about lunch. That's my news.
them: Seriously, though. You did it. You actually went. I told everyone at work. Nobody at work cares. I told them anyway.
them: Glad you went, kid. Someone in this family had to.
them: Right. Mum's waving at me to give her the receiver. She's not even on this call. Bye, kid.

== beat 2
tone: light
when: beat:home.jo.1
mem: office
flag: home.jo.office
them: Kid. Day four hundred and something at the office. I've named my stapler. It's called Steve. Steve's my best mate.   [laugh]
them: In my lunch hour I walk down to the water and watch them at the boatyard. They're building a boat. By hand.
them: Plank by plank. Bent with steam. It takes months. I've watched for three weeks. I've seen about four planks.
them: I've been carving a little one. On my desk. With a letter opener. It's terrible. It's very, very small.
them: I used to make things, didn't I. Shelves. That treehouse. The go-kart Mum still won't talk about.
them: Then I got a desk and stopped. Funny how that happens. Lunch is over. Back to Steve.

== beat 3
tone: heartfelt
when: beat:home.jo.2
mem: envy
flag: home.jo.envy
hint: Jo's calling late. It's the middle of the night where they are.
them: Can't sleep. Thought I'd ring. Good thing about this: you can't tell me to go to bed.
them: Can I say something stupid? I'm proud of you. And I'm a bit jealous. Both. At the same time.   [sigh]
them: Not of you. Of the going. You got on a shuttle. I got on the number nine to the office. Every day.
them: Mum drives it. She waves at me. I wave back. Then I sit down and do forms.
them: It's not your fault. It's not anyone's. I just needed to say it to someone who wouldn't make it a thing.
them: And you can't make it a thing. Because you can't talk. Perfect. Best listener I've got.   [laugh]
them: Right. Bed. Forms tomorrow. Night, kid. Don't tell Mum I rang this late.

== beat 4
tone: light
when: beat:home.jo.3
mem: sawdust
flag: home.jo.boatyard
them: Kid. I went in. To the boatyard. I asked if I could help on Saturdays. For nothing. Just to learn.   [laugh]
them: The old boatwright's called Wick. Wick looked at my hands and said "Those'll do." Best review I've ever had.
them: I sanded a plank for six hours. Best six hours of my life. Don't tell Steve.
them: We're building a rowing boat. Clinker-built. That means the planks overlap. I know words now. Boat words.
them: I've sawdust in places I didn't know I had. Mum made me shake out on the step. Gran laughed for an hour.
them: Monday tomorrow. Steve and I are not on speaking terms. I've moved on.

== beat 5
tone: serious
when: beat:home.jo.4
mem: quit
flag: home.jo.quit
hint: Jo's calling, and they sound like they've been pacing.
them: Kid. Wick's offered me an apprenticeship. A proper one. Four years. The money's half what the office pays.   [sigh]
them: I'd have to quit. Actually quit. With a letter. I've written the letter. It's in my pocket. It's been there a week.
them: Mum says do what makes me happy. Gran says "about time". I needed someone who'd just listen.
them: What if I'm too old? What if I'm no good at it? What if I quit and it's just... a different desk?
them: Then I thought: what would I tell you? Go. I'd tell you go. I did tell you go. When the letter came for the Wren.
them: That's not fair, kid. You didn't even say anything and you've won.   [laugh]
them: Right. I'm handing it in tomorrow. Before I lose my nerve. I'll bring sandwiches. Like a proper apprentice.

== beat 6
tone: resolution
when: beat:home.jo.5
mem: launch
flag: home.jo.launched
keepsake: a curl of wood from Jo's first boat
learn: Jo left the freight office to apprentice at the boatyard at home. Their first boat is called the Little Wren.
mail: 2880 | From Jo | A small envelope in the post bag. Inside, one curl of pale wood and a note: "From the Little Wren. First plank. Don't lose it, kid. Jo."
them: Kid. She floats. My first boat. In the water this morning. Wick let me push her off. She floats!   [laugh]
them: Mum cried. Gran stood on the jetty and shouted "Go on!" at a rowing boat. Everyone stared.
them: I named her. Don't laugh. The Little Wren. After your ship. Because you went first, and I followed. Sort of. Slowly.
them: It's painted on. In gold. Slightly wonky gold. Wick says wonky is how you know a person made it.
them: I've sent you a bit of her. A curl from the first plank. It's in the post bag. Don't lose it.
them: Thanks, kid. For going first. That's the whole of it. Thanks.

// ---- ordinary calls ----
== chat
// any time
them: Kid. Quick one. I'm in the queue at the chip shop. It's a long queue. You're my entertainment.
them: The man in front has ordered for his whole family. Nine. Nine portions. I've been counting.
them: I've decided they're a rowing team. That's the only explanation.
them: Oh, it's me. Bye, kid. Chips.
--
them: It's Jo. Mum's made me ring. Not made me. Suggested. Loudly. Over and over.
them: Nothing's happened. I've got no news. I'll tell you about my socks. They're odd. Both of them.
them: There. That's the call. Mum, I've rung. Happy? She's happy. Bye, kid.   [laugh]
--
them: Kid. I found your old bike in the shed. The blue one. With the bell that only rings sometimes.
them: I've oiled it. I don't know why. You're in space. You're not going to ride it.
them: It's ready, though. When you're back. Bell still only rings sometimes. Some things you don't fix.
them: Bye, kid.
--
them: Hello, kid. Rainy night. I'm on the windowsill with tea. Old habit. Your old habit, actually. I stole it.
them: You used to sit here and say you could see the stars through the rain. You couldn't. Nobody could.
them: Maybe you can now. Bye, kid.
--
them: Kid. Tell your ship I said hello. No, I mean it. Tell it. Mum talks to the bus. Gran talks to vegetables.
them: It's a family thing. Talking to things that can't talk back. Like you, on this call. Ha.   [laugh]
them: Bye, kid.
--
them: It's Jo. I'm at Mum's for tea. Gran's telling a story. It's the one about the goat. You know the goat.
them: She's changed the ending again. Now the goat wins. It never used to win.
them: I'm holding the receiver out so you can hear. Hear that? That's the goat winning. Bye, kid.
--
them: Kid. Your old friend Sam waved at me in town. Asked if you're eating. Everyone asks if you're eating.
them: I said you're probably eating better than us. They have a chef. We have Gran's crumble.
them: Bye, kid. Eat the chef's food. All of it.
--
when: time:night
them: Kid. It's late. Can't sleep. Not a big thing. Just one of those nights.
them: I like ringing you on these nights. You don't tell me to count sheep. You don't tell me anything.
them: I'm going to sit here a minute. You don't have to do anything. Just be on the line.
them: ...Right. Better. Night, kid.
--
them: Kid. Something stupid. I saw a shooting star tonight. Over the gasworks. I made a wish.
them: Not telling you what. That's the rules. But it was about you. Partly. Mostly about chips.   [laugh]
them: Bye, kid.
--
them: It's Jo. Do you remember the den? Under the stairs? With the torch and the tin of biscuits?
them: Mum's turned it into a cupboard. With actual things in it. Hoover. Ironing board. The end of an era.
them: I've left one biscuit in there. Behind the hoover. For tradition.
them: Bye, kid.
--
when: weekday:fri
them: Friday! The weekend's here. I'm going to do absolutely nothing. Professionally.
them: You get weekends up there? You must. Even space stops on a Saturday. Surely.
them: Do nothing for me. Bye, kid.
--
them: Kid. I've been thinking about the first thing you said when you could talk. You said "Jo." First word.
them: Mum says it was "more". Gran says it was "biscuit". It was "Jo". I was there. I'm sticking to it.
them: Bye, kid.   [laugh]

// after beat 1: the office, the boatyard not yet
--
when: beat=home.jo.1
them: Kid. Office update. Someone's moved my chair. Up. One notch. I've been sitting wrong all morning.
them: I've told the whole office. Nobody's owned up. There's a culprit among us.
them: This is my life now. Chair mysteries. You've got space. I've got a chair. Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.1
them: It's Jo. Lunch break. I've eaten my sandwich at my desk, like a sad person. Don't do that, up there.
them: Eat with people. Sit at a table. Even if you don't know them. Especially then.
them: That's big-sibling advice. Free of charge. Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.1
them: Kid. Form of the day: a man's sending a crate of pickled onions to his aunt. Four hundred jars.
them: I checked it. All correct. Four hundred jars. I'd like to meet that aunt.
them: That's the best thing that's happened all week. Pray for me. Bye, kid.   [laugh]

// after beat 2: watching the boatyard, carving
--
when: beat=home.jo.2
them: Kid. The boat's got ribs now. Like a whale. You can see right through it to the water.
them: I stood there so long one of them waved. Then went back to work. I went back to Steve.
them: Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.2
them: It's Jo. My little desk boat's got a name. It's called Steve Two. Steve's jealous.
them: My boss saw it. Said "What's that?" I said "A paperweight." He said "Good." Then he left.
them: It's not a paperweight. It's a boat. A tiny, wonky boat. Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.2
them: Kid. Gran asked why I smell of the river. I said lunch. She said you don't eat the river.
them: I said I watch it. She said "Ah." Very knowing. Gran knows everything. Don't tell her I said.
them: Bye, kid.

// after beat 3: after the honest night
--
when: beat=home.jo.3
them: Kid. Thanks for the other night. For listening. Well. For not being able to not listen.
them: I feel lighter. I don't know why saying it out loud helps. It just does.
them: I went past the boatyard again today. Didn't go in. Nearly did. Nearly's new.
them: Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.3
them: It's Jo. I bought a book on boats. A proper one. With drawings. I read it at my desk.
them: Behind a form. Like at school. Turns out I'm still very good at hiding books behind things.
them: Chapter four's about knots. I can do one. Badly. Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.3
them: Kid. I'm writing a list. Things I'd do if I wasn't scared. It's short. It's got one thing on it.
them: Not telling you what. You know what. Bye, kid.

// after beat 4: Saturdays at the yard, Wick
--
when: beat=home.jo.4
them: Kid. Wick taught me to steam a plank today. You put it in a box with steam and it goes soft. Like bread.
them: Then you bend it. Quick. Before it remembers it's wood. I bent one. It stayed bent. I'm a genius.
them: Bye, kid.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.jo.4
them: It's Jo. Wick doesn't talk much. Says three things a day. Today: "Good." "Again." and "Tea?"
them: I've never been happier with three words. Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.4
them: Kid. Steve's been moved to the drawer. I can't look at him. He knows what he did.
them: He did nothing. That's the problem. Five days of nothing, then Saturday. Saturday's everything.
them: Bye, kid.

// after beat 5: an apprentice now
--
when: beat=home.jo.5
them: Kid. I handed it in. The letter. My boss read it and said "Boats?" I said "Boats." He said "Fair."
them: I took Steve. Nobody said I couldn't. He's on the windowsill at the yard now. Retired.
them: Bye, kid. Apprentice Jo, signing off.
--
when: beat=home.jo.5
them: It's Jo. First week as a proper apprentice. My hands are wrecked. I've never been so pleased about anything.
them: Wick calls me "Apprentice". Not Jo. Like it's a rank. I'll take it.
them: Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.5
them: Kid. I make sandwiches now. Every morning. Cheese and pickle. Mum's delighted. She thinks I've grown up.
them: I haven't. I've just got less money. Same thing, Gran says.   [laugh]
them: Bye, kid.

// after beat 6: the Little Wren
--
when: beat=home.jo.6
them: Kid. I rowed the Little Wren out to the buoy and back. On my own. The whole way.
them: Halfway, I stopped and just sat. Water all round. Sky all round. Thought: this must be what it's like for you.
them: Bit smaller. Bit wetter. Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.6
them: It's Jo. Someone wants to buy a boat. Like the Little Wren. Wick says I can build it. Mostly me.
them: An order. A real one. My name on the paper. I didn't even check the form. That's growth.
them: Bye, kid.
--
when: beat=home.jo.6
them: Kid. Mum came out in the Little Wren. Gran wouldn't. Gran stood on the jetty and gave orders.
them: Mum rowed. In circles. For an hour. She says it's like the number nine, only wetter.
them: Best afternoon in years. Bye, kid.

// Gran's fall (Gran's beat 5): Jo on marrow duty
--
when: beat:home.gran.5 !beat:home.gran.6
them: Kid. I'm watering Gran's marrow. The Captain. Gran wrote me four pages. Four.
them: Page three is just "Talk to it. Kindly. It knows." So I talk to it. Kindly. It's a marrow.
them: Gran's all right. Cross. Very cross. That's how you know she's all right. Bye, kid.

// news: they've heard it in your messages
--
when: flag:intro.soloJob
them: Kid. Mum read out your message at tea. Your first job on your own. She cried into the gravy.
them: I'm not crying. I'm just saying: well done. Properly. You fixed a spaceship. I fix forms.
them: Bye, kid. Show-off.
--
when: cue:event.ionStorm.afterglow
them: Kid. A storm. In space. Mum's beside herself. I told her you're fine. You are fine?
them: Your message said loud. Loud's fine. Loud's just weather shouting. Bye, kid.
--
when: came:any
them: Kid. You went to the {trad}. Look at you. Going to things. You used to hide in the coats at parties.
them: I'm proud. Don't tell anyone. Bye, kid.
--
when: friend:any
them: Kid. {friend}, is it? Good. Tell {friend} I've got stories about you. Loads. They can have them cheap.
them: Glad you've got someone. Really. Bye, kid.   [laugh]
